WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.759
You don't have to read comics to love Thor:
Ragnarok, because director Taika Waititi and

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:07.779
his crew have made such a genuinely fun movie.

00:00:07.779 --> 00:00:11.290
But for longtime Marvel Comics fans, it's
even more special because of all the hidden

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.460
goodies inside.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:16.470
Here are some of the small references in Thor:
Ragnarok that you might have missed.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.760
And of course, spoilers ahead...

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.880
Immigrant Song

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.650
Led Zeppelin's "Immigrant Song" is the perfect
music to accompany a high-octane action sequence

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.840
like the ones at the beginning and end of
Thor: Ragnarok.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.020
Even better, the lyrics fit perfectly.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:34.180
Robert Plant and Jimmy Page wrote the song
after visiting Iceland and being impressed

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.610
with its landscape and history, and the song's
full of references to the same Scandinavian

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.350
mythology upon which the Thor comics were
based:

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.010
The line "The hammer of the gods" is a reference
to Thor's hammer Mjölnir.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.820
And of course when Robert Plant croons about
Valhalla, he's talking about the realm of

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:50.850
the honored warriors who fell in battle.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.980
The Norse afterlife is left out of the Thor
movies, but after the events of this one,

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.010
Valhalla would be crowded with Asgardians.

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.010
Korg and Miek

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:03.330
Korg is a Kronan, one of the Stone Men who
were Thor's very first enemies in his debut

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:07.120
appearance in the pages of Journey Into Mystery
#83.

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:11.380
Korg himself first appeared as an individual
character in the Planet Hulk comic book story

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.560
from 2006.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.060
That story also introduced Miek, a weird little
insect creature who turns out to be surprisingly

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.060
bloodthirsty.

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:23.460
In the movie, Miek doesn't speak at all, and
Korg — played by director Taika Waititi

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.979
— is much more soft-spoken than he seems
in the comic.

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:27.219
"It would pull me off.."

00:01:27.219 --> 00:01:29.299
"Oh my god, the hammer pulled you off?"

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:30.299
"The ground!

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:32.689
It pulled me off the ground up into the air
and I would fly."

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:34.170
They're fun characters.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:38.929
But if you only see the movie, you won't have
a clue about Miek's original nature as a character,

00:01:38.929 --> 00:01:42.090
or Korg's long comic book history with Thor.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.130
He's a Thing

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:46.729
Of course, Korg is far from the most famous
rock man in Marvel Comics.

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:51.470
That honor unquestionably goes to Ben Grimm,
the Fantastic Four member better known as

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:52.470
the Thing.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.079
The Thing has a long rivalry with the Hulk
in the comics, but he can't appear in movies

00:01:56.079 --> 00:01:58.259
with them because of licensing agreements.

00:01:58.259 --> 00:02:01.969
That's why it's funny when Thor first meets
Korg and takes him for a pile of rocks, and

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:05.039
Korg replies, saying, "I'm actually a thing,
a being."

00:02:05.039 --> 00:02:09.209
For fans of Marvel Comics, there's only one
image that pops in your head when a rock man

00:02:09.209 --> 00:02:10.929
introduces himself as a thing.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:12.390
This guy.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.049
"It's clobberin' time for me!"

00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:17.079
Point Break

00:02:17.079 --> 00:02:21.540
To activate the voice recognition security
system on the Quinjet, Thor has to refer to

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.530
himself as "Point Break," a call-back to the
nickname Tony Stark gave him in The Avengers.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:28.560
"No hard feelings, Point Break.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.069
You got a mean swing."

00:02:30.069 --> 00:02:34.829
Before his haircut at Stan Lee's hands, Thor
bore a pretty strong resemblance to Bodhi,

00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:39.700
the character Patrick Swayze plays in the
1991 film Point Break.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:40.930
Bannerless Hulk

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:45.390
Waking up as Bruce Banner after such a long
time as the Hulk is understandably jarring,

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:48.349
and it obviously has a negative impact on
Banner's psyche.

00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:51.019
This sort of thing has also happened in the
comics a few times.

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:55.469
During the Planet Hulk storyline, Banner rarely
appears because the planet is so dangerous.

00:02:55.469 --> 00:03:00.150
The most famous Bannerless Hulk storyline,
however, was published in 1988, in The Incredible

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.219
Hulk #347.

00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:06.459
Thanks to a wizard's spell, the Hulk is freed
of Banner, and gets a job working for the

00:03:06.459 --> 00:03:07.640
mob in Las Vegas.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:12.270
He even begins wearing tailored clothes in
his own size rather than Banner's torn pants,

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.120
and generally lives the high life.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.940
It's usually a mark of these "Bannerless Hulk"
stories that the Hulk speaks more and acts

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.440
more rationally the longer he stays himself,
so it makes sense that he's relatively talkative

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:23.519
in Ragnarok.

00:03:23.519 --> 00:03:24.810
Sokovia forgotten

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.310
Who even remembers Sokovia?

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.900
When Bruce Banner brings it up and asks what
happened to the city, Thor doesn't even answer

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.560
— he just points out that the battle with
Ultron happened two years ago.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.749
Sure, at the time, seeing a city lifted into
the sky by Ultron seemed like a huge Earth-shaking

00:03:39.749 --> 00:03:40.749
deal.

00:03:40.749 --> 00:03:44.300
It played a part in the events of Captain
America: Civil War, although even then, they

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.120
barely followed up on what happened there
after the battle.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:51.569
This exchange between Banner and Thor conveys
how long Banner has been out of it, but Thor's

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:55.659
reaction is also a bit of a joke about how
little the huge action sequences that end

00:03:55.659 --> 00:03:59.080
each movie tend to matter once the credits
roll.

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.080
Fenris

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:02.920
Ragnarok doesn't explain much about Fenris
the Wolf.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.459
He's treated mostly as an ally and mount for
the Death Goddess Hela.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:10.099
Interestingly, in the comics — and the Norse
myths on which they're based — Hela and

00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:14.680
Fenris are actually straight-up siblings,
and their father is none other than Loki.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:19.350
In the movie, Hela becomes Odin's daughter,
and therefore Loki's adopted sister, while

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.060
the wolf's origin is left completely unexplained.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.920
But the closeness between Hela and Fenris,
and that green glow in his eyes, hints at

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.920
their true relationship from the comics.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:29.940
Valkyrie's digs

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.950
She may look a little different, but the character
played by Tessa Thompson is very much the

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:38.320
same Valkyrie who has appeared in Marvel Comics
since the 1970s.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.350
Valkyrie's gold and silver armor and blue
cape come directly from the comics, along

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.530
with her sword, Dragonfang, and the flying
horse.

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:49.750
But more than anything, it's her characterization
that makes her recognizable.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:53.600
Valkyrie is a swaggering, hard-drinking warrior
woman who's worked hard to get away from her

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.860
long and troubled past, before eventually
making peace with it.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:04:58.860
Thor's eye

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.480
When Thor loses an eye in battle with Hela
and starts wearing an eyepatch at the end

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:06.350
of Thor: Ragnarok, it's an obvious way of
visually depicting his transition into his

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.440
father's role as King of Asgard, since Odin
was famously one-eyed as well.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:14.920
But it also resonates with a version of Thor
from the comics who bore an even closer resemblance

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:15.920
to Odin.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.920
King Thor, as he's often called, is a version
of Thor from the future who first appeared

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.390
in Thor, God of Thunder #1.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:26.000
Like so many things in Thor: Ragnarok, it
works perfectly well if you haven't read the

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.520
comics — but if you have, it takes on even
more meaning.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.630
Thanks for watching!

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:33.380
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.570
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

